       MS-DOS BOOT FROM USB is a free utility for creating bootable USB
	   drives (flash drives and external hard drives) from the MS-DOS 
	   operating system: 5.0, 6.22
       
                INSTRUCTIONS FOR USING A BOOTABLE USB DRIVE

       To boot from a bootable flash drive created in MS-DOS BOOT FROM 
	   USB on computers with UEFI (modern BIOS architecture), you need 
	   to disable Secure Boot in the BIOS/UEFI settings of the computer. 
	   The process of disabling varies depending on the manufacturer and
	   model of the motherboard, but, as a rule, you need to enter the
	   BIOS/UEFI during the initial boot (usually by pressing the Del, 
	   F2, F10, F12 or other key indicated on the boot screen) and find
	   the corresponding item in the settings menu.

       Important: after working with MS-DOS and turning off the computer
	   to start other operating systems, you may need to re-enable Secure
	   Boot to improve system security. For more information, see the
	   BIOS/UEFI instructions.

